 *   **Spatial Frequency Collapse:** When point clouds are sparse, a single neural descriptor is rasterized to multiple pixels and optimized to fit the average of multiple pixels, harming rendering quality [S1]. This is analogous to Nyquist Rate dissatisfaction [S1].
 *   **Neighbor Point Disturbance:** When point clouds are dense, points lying close have a larger impact on the feature optimization of each other [S1].
-*   **Sparsity/Noise:** Raw point clouds have noise $\epsilon$ such that $x_{pcd} = x_{obj} + \epsilon$. Feeding raw coordinates leads to spatial frequency collapse and blurred results because sparse points rasterize to multiple pixels, querying the same latent feature [S1]. Coordinate rectification solves this by providing uniformly distributed query points [S1].
-*   **Unoccupied Pixels:** Pixels with no points within radius $\tau$ cause artifacts (bleeding). These are handled by the refinement U-Net stage [S1].
-*   **Threshold Selection:** If $\tau$ is too large, rasterized points are inaccurate. If $\tau$ is too small, more pixels become unoccupied, causing bleeding [S1].
